"""HOOKS.json: the list of the hooks of each folder of the series, generated by the export and checked.

docs/STRUCTURE.md (HOOKS.json): every folder of SERIES that is not a backport
has a HOOKS.json beside its hooks.patch, one entry per hook (a backport folder has one for the hooks of its files that are not upstream code, category `backport`). The
export (and `python tools/engine.py hooks-list`, which is the same function, `write_all`) writes it from the hooks.patch; `check` fails when
the committed file and the series differ, so a hook lost in a merge does not pass silently.

An entry, in this order of keys:

    file      the Mozilla file
    function  the host function (git's hunk header)
    kind      add | rewrite | delete | file   (a deletion and a whole-file removal are listed too: nothing is invisible)
    form      guard | notice | wrap-expression | wrap-call | wrap-function | declared-value | registration | member | shadow | scope | rewire |
              parameter | anchor | excepted | unknown (`unknown` is rule C21; `excepted` is written for an `unknown` line whose entry has the
              complete exception row, see below)
    line      the added text ('' for a deletion)
    removed   the Mozilla line a rewrite replaces, or what a deletion takes away (left out for a pure addition)
    ---- written by hand, kept by the export ----
    test      the test that proves the hook ('' while there is none: that is debt, rule T1 of hook_contract)
    anchor    (form anchor only) the Mozilla statement the hook follows
    why       (form anchor only) why the guard, wrap-function and wrap-expression forms are not enough (rule T2)
    exception (G35, only when the pm granted it) why no form can express this rewritten line
    reason_kind (port-06, with `exception` on a rewrite) the kind of the reason: expression | declaration | macro | skip
              (tools/hook_contract.py C12..C15 judge a rewrite by it; the exception text says why). A line that no form takes and whose
              entry has `exception`, `reason_kind` and `pm` all three is written with the form `excepted` (G63); with one missing it stays
              `unknown` and rule C21 says which
    quiet_anchor_why  (port-07, a hook whose hunk is in the churn band of tools/churn.py) why no quieter anchor serves:
              {"kind": "only-place", "why": "<words>"} or {"kind": "deferred", "quieter": "<mozilla file>:<anchor text>"}
              (`deferred` only while tools/churn.json is for the series' tag); rule C18 of tools/hook_contract.py judges it
    untestable  (only when the pm granted it) why the hook has no test: `test` stays '' and rule T1 does not apply
    pm        exactly "approvato": the pm granted the exception (G35) or the untestable. `exception` and `pm` make a
              declared exception: the hook is left out of contract_violations (its C-rules C1..C11, T1, T2, T3; not C12..C15,
              which judge the exception itself) and counted
              apart by `check`; `untestable` and `pm` leave out T1 only, counted apart too. `exception` or `untestable`
              without `pm: "approvato"` is a failure of `check`, and so is `untestable` beside a `test`

Identity of a hook: file + function + kind + normalized line text (+ normalized removed text). The export
merges the hand fields into the generated list by identity; a hook whose text changes loses them on
purpose, and `check` asks for them again.

Test references (the grammar of `test`; '' is allowed and is debt):

    gtest:<Suite>.<Name>            some file under any folder's src/ has TEST(<Suite>, <Name>) or TEST_F(<Suite>, <Name>)
    node:<path>[#<test title>]      <path> is a tree path (juggler/tests/x.test.mjs): <folder>/src/<path> exists in some
                                    folder; with a title, the title string appears in the file
    py:<path>::<test_name>          the same for a python test of ours: the file exists and defines test_name; a path under
                                    tools/tests/ is a test of the series itself, read from the series root
    py:<path>::invariant:<name>     a check that is not a test function (an invariant of a checker script): the file exists
    py:<path>::case:<name>          and contains the name as a word (a string or an identifier), the same for a case name
    xpcshell:<path>#<test>          the same for an xpcshell test: the file exists and the title is in it (rule T3: no
                                    round runs xpcshell yet, so it is debt until a gate leg does)
    cargo:<crate>::<test path>      a rust test of a crate of ours (`cargo:stealth_x::tests::it_works`): only the format is
                                    checked here; a round must run `cargo test` for the crate (rule T3)
    wrapper:<path>::<test>          a test of the wrapper repository: only the format is checked here; <test> may be
                                    invariant:<name> or case:<name> for a check of a script that is not a test function

A test must also be RUN by a round (rule T3, G20), judged on the series' copy of the candidate workflow
(platform/ci/src/.github/workflows/release.yml, not judged when the series has none):

    gtest:<Suite>.<Name>   the suite is in the gtest filter (the `gtest_filter` input's default), by name or by a wildcard
    node:<path>            the node job runs it: <path> matches a glob of a `node --test` line (juggler/tests/*.test.mjs)
    py:<path>::<name>      some step of a workflow runs the file, or it is a tree python test of the export gate
                           (modules/libpref/test/test_stealth_*.py)
    cargo:<crate>          a step of a workflow runs `cargo test` for the crate (`-p <crate>`, `--package <crate>`, a
                           `--manifest-path` of it, or `--workspace`/`--all`), as the cargo job of cargo-tests.yml does
    wrapper:               the wrapper's own rounds run it: format only
    xpcshell:              run by no round, until a workflow line runs that file with xpcshell

Public API:

    generated(root, folder)           -> list[dict]   the entries the series generates for one folder
    merge(generated, committed)       -> list[dict]   generated entries with the hand fields of the committed ones
    expected(root, folder)            -> list[dict]   merge(generated, the committed file)
    render(entries)                   -> str          the text of a HOOKS.json
    write_all(root)                   -> dict         writes every HOOKS.json, folder -> number of entries
    problems(root)                    -> list[Failure]   what `check` reports (each one built by fail(): tools/failure.py)
    reference_problem(root, ref)      -> Failure | None  why a test reference does not resolve
    missing_test_violations(root)     -> list[Violation]   rules T1, T2 and T3 for hook_contract
    declared_exceptions(root)         -> list[(folder, entry)]   the entries with `exception` and `pm: "approvato"` (G35)
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import fnmatch
import json
import re
import shlex
from pathlib import Path

import hook_contract as hc
import layout
from failure import Failure, fail

# The decisions the messages of this file name: literals, because the scan of tools/tests/test_failure_messages.py reads a literal or a
# constant of the file; tools/tests/test_hook_rules_table.py checks they are the ones of hook_contract.
D_HOOK02 = "hook-02-shape-and-contract"
D_HOOK04 = "hook-04-one-test-per-hook"
D_PORT06 = "port-06-hooks-only-add"
HAND_FIELDS = ("test", "anchor", "why", "exception", "reason_kind", "untestable", "pm", "quiet_anchor_why")
PM_APPROVED = "approvato"
WORKFLOWS = "platform/ci/src/.github/workflows"
CANDIDATE_WORKFLOW = "release.yml"
TREE_PY_TESTS = "modules/libpref/test/test_stealth_*.py"       # run by the export gate (tools/export_gate.py)
GTEST_RE = re.compile(r"\bTEST(?:_F)?\s*\(\s*(\w+)\s*,\s*(\w+)\s*\)")
GTEST_SUFFIXES = {".cpp", ".cc", ".cxx", ".h", ".hpp"}
WRAPPER_RE = re.compile(r"^wrapper:[^\s:#]+::\S+$")
CARGO_RE = re.compile(r"^cargo:([A-Za-z0-9_\-]+)::(\w+(?:::\w+)*)$")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the folders
def series_names(root: Path) -> list:
    return (Path(root) / "SERIES").read_text(encoding="utf-8").split()


def folders(root: Path) -> list:
    """The folders of SERIES that carry a HOOKS.json: every folder that is not a backport, and a backport whose hooks.patch has a file
    that is not upstream code (category `backport`: vendored, its own rules): the hooks of those files are listed, the upstream ones are not."""
    root = Path(root)
    return [f for f in series_names(root) if (root / f).is_dir()
            and (not (root / f / "BACKPORT.json").exists() or "diff --git " in hc.own_patch_text(root, f))]


def list_path(root: Path, folder: str) -> Path:
    return Path(root) / folder / "HOOKS.json"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- generating
def _squeeze(t: str) -> str:
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", t.strip())


def identity(e: dict) -> tuple:
    return (e["file"], _squeeze(e["function"]), e["kind"], hc.norm(e["line"]), hc.norm(e.get("removed", "")))


def _entry(h: hc.Hook) -> dict:
    e = {"file": h.file, "function": h.function, "kind": h.kind, "form": h.form, "line": h.line}
    if h.removed:
        e["removed"] = h.removed
    return e


def generated(root: Path, folder: str) -> list:
    """One entry per hook of `folder`, in the order of its hooks.patch: file, function, kind, form, line (and
    removed). [] for a folder without a hooks.patch. The files of category `backport` (upstream code) have no entry."""
    root = Path(root)
    hooks, _ = hc.analyse_patch(folder, hc.own_patch_text(root, folder))
    return [_entry(h) for h in hooks]


def _with_hand_fields(e: dict, old: dict | None) -> dict:
    out = dict(e)
    old = old or {}
    out["test"] = old.get("test", "")
    if e["form"] == "anchor":
        out["anchor"] = old.get("anchor", "")
        out["why"] = old.get("why", "")
    if e["form"] != "anchor":        # an anchor declared on the last statement of a block (G23, G30): kept where written
        for k in ("anchor", "why"):
            if old.get(k):
                out[k] = old[k]
    for k in ("exception", "reason_kind", "untestable", "pm"):          # the pm's decisions (G35): only where they were written
        if k in old:
            out[k] = old[k]
    if "quiet_anchor_why" in old:        # port-07: why no quieter anchor serves (a hook in the churn band); the shape is C18's to judge
        out["quiet_anchor_why"] = old["quiet_anchor_why"]
    if out["form"] == "unknown" and not hc.excepted_lacks(out):
        out["form"] = "excepted"         # G63: no form takes the line, and its exception row is complete
    return out


def merge(gen: list, committed: list) -> list:
    """The generated entries, each with the hand fields (test, anchor, why, exception, pm) of the committed entry of the same
    identity; none where there is none. Two entries of one identity are paired in order."""
    pool = {}
    for c in committed or []:
        if isinstance(c, dict) and all(k in c for k in ("file", "function", "kind", "line")):
            pool.setdefault(identity(c), []).append(c)
    out = []
    for e in gen:
        same = pool.get(identity(e))
        out.append(_with_hand_fields(e, same.pop(0) if same else None))
    return out


def load(root: Path, folder: str):
    """The committed list of a folder, or None if there is no file."""
    path = list_path(root, folder)
    if not path.is_file():
        return None
    return json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))


def expected(root: Path, folder: str) -> list:
    return merge(generated(root, folder), load(root, folder) or [])


def render(entries: list) -> str:
    return json.dumps(entries, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n"


def committed_entries(root: Path) -> dict:
    """{folder: committed entries} of every HOOKS.json on disk under the series (a folder that is in SERIES or not): the hand fields the
    export must not lose when it moves a hook's line to another folder or removes a folder. A file that is not a list is left out."""
    root, found = Path(root), {}
    for folder in sorted(set(layout.folders_on_disk(root)) | set(series_names(root) if (root / "SERIES").is_file() else [])):
        path = list_path(root, folder)
        if path.is_file():
            data = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
            if isinstance(data, list):
                found[folder] = data
    return found


def _by_identity(entries: list) -> dict:
    pool = {}
    for c in entries or []:
        if isinstance(c, dict) and all(k in c for k in ("file", "function", "kind", "line")):
            pool.setdefault(identity(c), []).append(c)
    return pool


def _loose(e: dict) -> tuple:
    """An entry without its host function: file, kind, normalized line and removed text."""
    return (e["file"], e["kind"], hc.norm(e["line"]), hc.norm(e.get("removed", "")))


def merge_all(generated_by_folder: dict, committed_by_folder: dict) -> dict:
    """{folder: entries}: each generated entry with the hand fields of the committed entry of the same identity, found first in the
    folder's own committed list (as `merge` does), then in a fallback pool of every entry that no folder's own list used, over all
    committed lists in folder order: a hook whose line moved to another folder keeps its hand fields (test, anchor, why, exception,
    reason_kind, pm, quiet_anchor_why, untestable). An entry is used once."""
    own = {f: _by_identity(committed_by_folder.get(f)) for f in generated_by_folder}
    matched = {f: [None] * len(gen) for f, gen in generated_by_folder.items()}
    for f, gen in generated_by_folder.items():
        for i, e in enumerate(gen):
            same = own[f].get(identity(e))
            if same:
                matched[f][i] = same.pop(0)
    spare = {}
    for f in sorted(committed_by_folder):
        pool = own[f] if f in own else _by_identity(committed_by_folder[f])
        for ident, entries in pool.items():
            spare.setdefault(ident, []).extend(entries)
    for f, gen in generated_by_folder.items():
        for i, e in enumerate(gen):
            if matched[f][i] is None:
                same = spare.get(identity(e))
                if same:
                    matched[f][i] = same.pop(0)
    # A hunk header (`function`) is git's choice of a line above the hunk, and the line above a hook is another folder's hook once the
    # file is split, so the header of a hook can change while the hook does not: the leftover entries are matched once more without it, where
    # the pairing is not a guess (as many leftover entries as hooks to give them to, for that file, kind and text).
    loose = {}
    for entries in spare.values():
        for c in entries:
            loose.setdefault(_loose(c), []).append(c)
    wanting = {}
    for f, gen in generated_by_folder.items():
        for i, e in enumerate(gen):
            if matched[f][i] is None:
                wanting.setdefault(_loose(e), []).append((f, i))
    for key, places in wanting.items():
        if len(loose.get(key, [])) == len(places):
            for (f, i), c in zip(places, loose[key]):
                matched[f][i] = c
    return {f: [_with_hand_fields(e, matched[f][i]) for i, e in enumerate(gen)] for f, gen in generated_by_folder.items()}


def write_all(root: Path, pool: dict | None = None) -> dict:
    """Writes the HOOKS.json of every folder (the export and `hooks-list` both come here). -> folder -> entries. `pool` is
    committed_entries(root) taken before the run (the export takes it before it removes or writes a folder); without it, the lists on disk
    now."""
    root = Path(root)
    pool = committed_entries(root) if pool is None else pool
    names = folders(root)
    merged = merge_all({f: generated(root, f) for f in names}, pool)
    counts = {}
    for folder in names:
        list_path(root, folder).write_text(render(merged[folder]), encoding="utf-8", newline="")
        counts[folder] = len(merged[folder])
    return counts


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- test references
class _Index:
    """What the folders' src/ trees hold, read once and only when a reference needs it."""

    def __init__(self, root: Path):
        self.root, self._gtests = Path(root), None

    def src_file(self, path: str):
        for folder in series_names(self.root):
            p = self.root / folder / "src" / path
            if p.is_file():
                return p
        return None

    def gtests(self) -> set:
        if self._gtests is None:
            found = set()
            for folder in series_names(self.root):
                src = self.root / folder / "src"
                if not src.is_dir():
                    continue
                for p in src.rglob("*"):
                    if p.suffix in GTEST_SUFFIXES and p.is_file():
                        text = p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
                        if "TEST" in text:
                            found.update(GTEST_RE.findall(text))
            self._gtests = found
        return self._gtests


def _where(folder: str, e: dict | None = None) -> str:
    """Where a problem is: the folder's HOOKS.json, and the file and host function of the hook when it is about one."""
    if not e:
        return "%s/HOOKS.json" % folder
    function = _squeeze(e.get("function", ""))
    return "%s/HOOKS.json, %s%s" % (folder, e.get("file", "?"), ", " + function if function else "")


def _ref_failure(where: str, ref: str, why: str, fix: str) -> Failure:
    return fail(where, "the test `%s` does not resolve: %s" % (ref, why), fix, D_HOOK04)


def _title_in(text: str, title: str) -> bool:
    """The title of a node test is in the file: as it is, or as a JavaScript string literal writes it (a quote of its own quote kind escaped)."""
    return any(c in text for c in (title, title.replace("'", "\\'"), title.replace('"', '\\"'), title.replace("`", "\\`")))


def reference_problem(root: Path, ref: str, index: _Index | None = None, where: str | None = None) -> Failure | None:
    """None if the test reference resolves, else the failure that says why it does not (grammar in the module docstring);
    `where` is the hook's place in HOOKS.json (`_where`)."""
    index = index or _Index(root)
    where = where or "the `test` of a HOOKS.json entry"
    grammar = "docs/STRUCTURE.md, test references"
    kind, _, rest = ref.partition(":")
    if kind == "gtest":
        suite, dot, name = rest.partition(".")
        if not (suite and dot and name) or not re.fullmatch(r"\w+", suite) or not re.fullmatch(r"\w+", name):
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "it is not gtest:<Suite>.<Name>", "write gtest:<Suite>.<Name>, each a word of letters, digits and underscores")
        if (suite, name) in index.gtests():
            return None
        return _ref_failure(where, ref, "no TEST(%s, %s) or TEST_F(%s, %s) in any folder's src/" % (suite, name, suite, name),
                            "write that gtest under the src/ of a folder, or name a gtest that exists")
    if kind == "node":
        path, _, title = rest.partition("#")
        if not path:
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "it is not node:<path>[#<title>]", "write node:<path>[#<title>] (%s)" % grammar)
        p = index.src_file(path)
        if p is None:
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "%s is in no folder's src/" % path, "name a path that exists as <folder>/src/%s, or write the test there" % path)
        if title and not _title_in(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace"), title):
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "the title %r is not in %s" % (title, path), "name a title that the file has, or add the test with that title")
        return None
    if kind == "py":
        path, sep, name = rest.partition("::")
        if not (path and sep and name):
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "it is not py:<path>::<test_name>", "write py:<path>::<test_name> (%s)" % grammar)
        p = index.src_file(path)
        if p is None and path.startswith("tools/tests/") and (Path(root) / path).is_file():
            p = Path(root) / path           # a test of the series itself
        if p is None:
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "%s is in no folder's src/" % path, "name a python file that exists as <folder>/src/%s or under tools/tests/" % path)
        text = p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
        label, colon, bare = name.partition(":")
        if colon and label in ("invariant", "case"):
            if not bare:
                return _ref_failure(where, ref, "it is not py:<path>::%s:<name>" % label, "write the name of the %s after the colon" % label)
            if not re.search(r"(?<!\w)%s(?!\w)" % re.escape(bare), text):
                return _ref_failure(where, ref, "%s has no %s named %s" % (path, label, bare), "name a %s that the file has" % label)
            return None
        if not re.search(r"\bdef\s+%s\b" % re.escape(name), text):
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "%s does not define %s" % (path, name), "name a test function the file defines, or write %s there" % name)
        return None
    if kind == "xpcshell":
        path, sep, title = rest.partition("#")
        if not (path and sep and title):
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "it is not xpcshell:<path>#<test>", "write xpcshell:<path>#<test> (%s)" % grammar)
        p = index.src_file(path)
        if p is None:
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "%s is in no folder's src/" % path, "name a path that exists as <folder>/src/%s" % path)
        if title not in p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace"):
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "the title %r is not in %s" % (title, path), "name a title that the file has")
        return None
    if kind == "wrapper":
        if not WRAPPER_RE.match(ref):
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "it is not wrapper:<path>::<test>", "write wrapper:<path>::<test> (%s)" % grammar)
        label, colon, bare = rest.partition("::")[2].partition(":")
        if colon and label in ("invariant", "case") and not bare:
            return _ref_failure(where, ref, "it is not wrapper:<path>::%s:<name>" % label, "write the name of the %s after the colon" % label)
        return None
    if kind == "cargo":
        if CARGO_RE.match(ref):
            return None
        return _ref_failure(where, ref, "it is not cargo:<crate>::<full test path>", "write cargo:<crate>::<full test path> (%s)" % grammar)
    return _ref_failure(where, ref, "its kind %r is none of gtest:, node:, py:, xpcshell:, cargo:, wrapper:" % kind,
                        "start the reference with one of them (%s)" % grammar)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the check
def _label(e: dict) -> str:
    """The hook's text, whole: its added line, or `<kind> removed text` for a hook that adds none."""
    return e.get("line") or ("<%s> %s" % (e.get("kind", "?"), _squeeze(e.get("removed", ""))))


def _diff(folder: str, gen: list, committed: list) -> list:
    """The differences between the committed list and the generation (hand fields are not compared)."""
    found, pool = [], {}
    regen = "run `python tools/engine.py hooks-list`: it writes the list from hooks.patch and keeps the hand fields"
    for c in committed:
        pool.setdefault(identity(c), []).append(c)
    matched = []
    for e in gen:
        same = pool.get(identity(e))
        if not same:
            found.append(fail(_where(folder, e), "HOOKS.json lacks the hook `%s`, which hooks.patch adds" % _label(e), regen, D_HOOK02))
            continue
        c = same.pop(0)
        matched.append(c)
        for k in ("form", "kind", "removed", "line", "function", "file"):
            if c.get(k, "") != e.get(k, ""):
                found.append(fail(_where(folder, e), "the hook `%s` has %s %r in HOOKS.json, the series generates %r"
                                  % (_label(e), k, c.get(k, ""), e.get(k, "")), regen, D_HOOK02))
    for rest in pool.values():
        for c in rest:
            found.append(fail(_where(folder, c), "HOOKS.json lists the hook `%s`, which hooks.patch no longer has" % _label(c), regen, D_HOOK02))
    return found


def exception_problems(folder: str, e: dict) -> list:
    """G35: an entry with `exception` needs the reason (a non-empty string) and `pm: "approvato"`; a `pm` that is
    not exactly "approvato" is wrong whatever the entry says."""
    found = []
    where, label = _where(folder, e), _label(e)
    grant = "ask the pm for the approval and set `pm` to \"%s\"; without it, delete the field" % PM_APPROVED
    if "exception" in e and not (isinstance(e["exception"], str) and e["exception"].strip()):
        found.append(fail(where, "the exception of the hook `%s` has no reason (a non-empty text)" % label,
                          "write why no form can express this rewritten line, or delete the `exception` (G35)", D_PORT06))
    if "exception" in e and e.get("pm") != PM_APPROVED:
        found.append(fail(where, "the hook `%s` declares an exception without `pm: \"%s\"`: only the pm grants one (G35)" % (label, PM_APPROVED),
                          grant, D_PORT06))
    elif "pm" in e and e["pm"] != PM_APPROVED:
        found.append(fail(where, "the hook `%s` has pm %r, which is not \"%s\"" % (label, e["pm"], PM_APPROVED), grant, D_PORT06))
    if e.get("form") == "excepted" and hc.excepted_lacks(e):
        found.append(fail(where, "C21: the hook `%s` has the form `excepted` and its exception row is incomplete, it lacks %s" % (label, ", ".join(hc.excepted_lacks(e))),
                          "write the missing fields of the row (the reason in `exception`, `reason_kind`, and the pm's `pm`), or make the line one of the forms", D_HOOK02))
    if "reason_kind" in e:
        if e["reason_kind"] not in hc.REASON_KINDS:
            found.append(fail(where, "the reason_kind of the hook `%s` is %r, not one of %s" % (label, e["reason_kind"], "/".join(hc.REASON_KINDS)),
                              "set `reason_kind` to one of %s" % "/".join(hc.REASON_KINDS), D_PORT06))
        if "exception" not in e:
            found.append(fail(where, "the hook `%s` has a reason_kind and no exception: it qualifies the exception" % label,
                              "add the `exception` (and the pm's `pm`), or delete the `reason_kind`", D_PORT06))
    if "untestable" in e:
        if not (isinstance(e["untestable"], str) and e["untestable"].strip()):
            found.append(fail(where, "the untestable of the hook `%s` has no reason (a non-empty text)" % label,
                              "write why the hook cannot be tested, or delete the field and write the test", D_HOOK04))
        if e.get("pm") != PM_APPROVED:
            found.append(fail(where, "the hook `%s` is untestable without `pm: \"%s\"`: only the pm grants it" % (label, PM_APPROVED),
                              grant, D_HOOK04))
        if e.get("test"):
            found.append(fail(where, "the hook `%s` is untestable and names a test: one or the other" % label,
                              "delete `untestable` (the hook has a test) or delete `test`", D_HOOK04))
    return found


def is_declared_exception(e: dict) -> bool:
    """G35: the entry has a reason and the pm's approval."""
    return isinstance(e.get("exception"), str) and bool(e["exception"].strip()) and e.get("pm") == PM_APPROVED


def is_untestable(e: dict) -> bool:
    """The entry has a reason, the pm's approval and no test: rule T1 does not apply to it."""
    return (isinstance(e.get("untestable"), str) and bool(e["untestable"].strip()) and e.get("pm") == PM_APPROVED
            and not e.get("test"))


def untestable_hooks(root: Path) -> list:
    """[(folder, entry)] of every hook of the series that the pm declared untestable."""
    return [(f, e) for f in folders(root) for e in expected(root, f) if is_untestable(e)]


def declared_exceptions(root: Path) -> list:
    """[(folder, entry)] of every hook of the series that is a declared exception (merged with the committed list)."""
    return [(f, e) for f in folders(root) for e in expected(root, f) if is_declared_exception(e)]


def problems(root: Path) -> list:
    """What `check` reports, as a list of Failure: a folder whose committed HOOKS.json differs from what its hooks.patch generates
    (missing file, a hook missing or extra, a field changed, the order) and a `test` that names a test that does not exist."""
    root, found = Path(root), []
    index = _Index(root)
    regen = "run `python tools/engine.py hooks-list`: it writes the list from hooks.patch and keeps the hand fields"
    for folder in folders(root):
        try:
            committed = load(root, folder)
        except ValueError as exc:
            found.append(fail(_where(folder), "it is not JSON: %s" % exc,
                              "restore the file from git (`git checkout -- %s/HOOKS.json`), then run `python tools/engine.py hooks-list`" % folder,
                              D_HOOK02))
            continue
        if committed is None:
            found.append(fail(_where(folder), "it is missing", regen, D_HOOK02))
            continue
        if not isinstance(committed, list) or not all(isinstance(c, dict) and all(k in c for k in ("file", "function", "kind", "line"))
                                                      for c in committed):
            found.append(fail(_where(folder), "it is not a list of hook entries (each with file, function, kind and line)", regen, D_HOOK02))
            continue
        gen = generated(root, folder)
        diff = _diff(folder, merge(gen, committed), committed)         # the form of an excepted line comes from its hand fields
        found += diff
        if not diff and render(merge(gen, committed)) != render(committed):
            found.append(fail(_where(folder), "it differs from what the series generates (the order of the entries or of their keys)", regen, D_HOOK02))
        for c in committed:
            found += exception_problems(folder, c)
            ref = c.get("test", "")
            if not isinstance(ref, str):
                found.append(fail(_where(folder, c), "the test of the hook `%s` is %r, not a string" % (_label(c), ref),
                                  "write the reference as a text, e.g. py:<path>::<name> (docs/STRUCTURE.md, test references)", D_HOOK04))
            elif ref:
                why = reference_problem(root, ref, index, _where(folder, c))
                if why:
                    found.append(why)
    return found


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- rounds (rule T3)
class _Rounds:
    """What the rounds run, read from the series' copy of the workflows: the gtest filter of the candidate, its
    `node --test` globs, and the lines of every workflow that run a python file. `known` is False when the series
    has no copy of the candidate workflow: nothing can then be judged."""

    def __init__(self, root: Path):
        wf = Path(root) / WORKFLOWS
        self.known = (wf / CANDIDATE_WORKFLOW).is_file()
        self.filter, self.node_globs, self.lines, self.cargo_tests = "", [], [], []
        self.xpcshell_files = self._xpcshell_list(Path(root))
        if not self.known:
            return
        cand = (wf / CANDIDATE_WORKFLOW).read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").split("\n")
        self.filter = self._gtest_filter(cand)
        for p in sorted(wf.glob("*.yml")):          # the node tests run in checks.yml, which the PR round and the candidate both call
            self.node_globs += self._node_globs(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines())
        for p in sorted(wf.glob("*.yml")):
            lines = [ln for ln in p.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").split("\n")
                     if not ln.lstrip().startswith("#")]
            self.lines += lines
            joined = ""             # a command continued with a backslash is one command
            workdir = ""            # the `working-directory:` of the step the line is in, "" if it has none
            for ln in lines:
                if re.match(r"^\s*-\s+[\w\-]+:", ln):
                    workdir = ""    # a list item of mappings: a new step
                wd = re.match(r"""^\s*(?:-\s+)?working-directory:\s*(['"]?)([^'"\s]+)\1\s*$""", ln)
                if wd:
                    workdir = wd.group(2)
                joined += ln.rstrip()
                if joined.endswith("\\"):
                    joined = joined[:-1] + " "
                    continue
                if re.search(r"\bcargo\s+(?:\+\S+\s+)?(?:test|nextest)\b", joined):
                    self.cargo_tests.append((joined, workdir))
                joined = ""

    def _node_globs(self, lines: list) -> list:
        """The file patterns the `node --test` lines of a workflow run, a `$f` standing for the globs of its `for` loop until `done`."""
        found = []
        loops = {}              # `for f in juggler/tests/*.test.mjs; do`: the globs a `$f` stands for, until `done`
        for ln in lines:
            code = ln.split(" #", 1)[0]
            if ln.lstrip().startswith("#"):
                continue
            loop = re.match(r"^\s*for\s+(\w+)\s+in\s+(.+?);\s*do\b", code)
            if loop:
                loops[loop.group(1)] = self._words(loop.group(2))
            elif re.match(r"^\s*done\b", code):
                loops.clear()
            m = re.search(r"\bnode\s+--test\s+(.+)$", code)
            if m:
                for a in self._words(m.group(1), stop=True):
                    if a.startswith("-"):
                        continue
                    if a.startswith("$"):
                        found += loops.get(a.strip("${}"), [])
                    else:
                        found.append(a)
        return found

    @staticmethod
    def _words(text: str, stop: bool = False) -> list:
        """The shell words of `text`; with `stop`, up to the first `||`, `&&` or `;`."""
        lex = shlex.shlex(text, posix=True, punctuation_chars=True)
        lex.whitespace_split = True
        out = []
        try:
            for w in lex:
                if stop and w in ("||", "&&", ";", "|", "&"):
                    break
                out.append(w)
        except ValueError:
            pass
        return out

    @staticmethod
    def _xpcshell_list(root: Path) -> set:
        """The files the xpcshell leg runs: the `files` list of scripts/ci/xpcshell_tests.json of the series' copy of the CI."""
        p = root / "platform" / "ci" / "src" / "scripts" / "ci" / "xpcshell_tests.json"
        try:
            data = json.loads(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            return set()
        files = data.get("files", []) if isinstance(data, dict) else data
        return {f for f in files if isinstance(f, str)}

    @staticmethod
    def _gtest_filter(lines: list) -> str:
        """The default of the `gtest_filter` input: the first `default:` under a key `gtest_filter:`."""
        for i, ln in enumerate(lines):
            m = re.match(r"^(\s*)gtest_filter:\s*$", ln)
            if not m:
                continue
            for nxt in lines[i + 1:]:
                if nxt.strip() and len(nxt) - len(nxt.lstrip()) <= len(m.group(1)):
                    break
                d = re.match(r"^\s*default:\s*(['\"])(.*)\1\s*$", nxt)
                if d:
                    return d.group(2)
        return ""

    def gtest_runs(self, suite: str, name: str) -> bool:
        pos, _, neg = self.filter.partition("-")
        full = "%s.%s" % (suite, name)
        hit = lambda patterns: any(fnmatch.fnmatchcase(full, q) for q in patterns.split(":") if q)
        return hit(pos or "*") and not hit(neg)

    def node_runs(self, path: str) -> bool:
        for g in self.node_globs:
            rx = "".join("[^/]*" if ch == "*" else "[^/]" if ch == "?" else re.escape(ch) for ch in g)
            if re.fullmatch(rx, path):
                return True
        return False

    def py_runs(self, path: str) -> bool:
        if fnmatch.fnmatchcase(path, TREE_PY_TESTS):
            return True
        pat = re.compile(r"(?<![\w.\-])%s(?![\w])" % re.escape(path))
        return any(pat.search(ln) or self._pytest_directory(ln, path) for ln in self.lines)

    @staticmethod
    def _pytest_directory(line: str, path: str) -> bool:
        """A `pytest` line with a plain argument (no `-`, no `$`) that is a directory the test file is under."""
        if not re.search(r"\bpytest\b", line):
            return False
        for tok in line.split():
            tok = tok.strip("\"'").rstrip("/")
            if tok and not tok.startswith(("-", "$")) and "/" in tok and path.startswith(tok + "/"):
                return True
        return False

    def cargo_runs(self, crate: str) -> bool:
        """A `cargo test` names the crate (`-p`, `--package`, a `--manifest-path` of it), runs the whole workspace, or
        names no package and runs in its step's `working-directory`, a directory called as the crate: cargo then tests
        the package of the manifest there (the mdns_service step of cargo-tests.yml)."""
        for cmd, workdir in self.cargo_tests:
            if re.search(r"--workspace\b|--all\b", cmd):
                return True
            if re.search(r"(?:^|\s)(?:-p|--package)[\s=]+%s(?![\w\-])" % re.escape(crate), cmd):
                return True
            if re.search(r"--manifest-path[\s=]+\S*(?<![\w\-])%s/Cargo\.toml" % re.escape(crate), cmd):
                return True
            names_a_package = re.search(r"(?:^|\s)(?:-p|--package|--manifest-path)(?:[\s=]|$)", cmd)
            if workdir and not names_a_package and workdir.rstrip("/").rsplit("/", 1)[-1] == crate:
                return True
        return False

    def xpcshell_runs(self, path: str) -> bool:
        pat = re.compile(r"(?<![\w.\-])%s(?![\w])" % re.escape(path))
        return path in self.xpcshell_files or any("xpcshell" in ln and pat.search(ln) for ln in self.lines)

    def runs(self, ref: str):
        """True/False if a round runs the test `ref` names; None when it cannot be judged (no workflow, a format
        that is not one of gtest/node/py, a malformed reference: `problems` reports those)."""
        kind, _, rest = ref.partition(":")
        if kind == "xpcshell":      # no round runs xpcshell yet: debt until a workflow line does
            path = rest.partition("#")[0]
            return self.xpcshell_runs(path) if path else None
        if not self.known:
            return None
        if kind == "gtest":
            suite, dot, name = rest.partition(".")
            return self.gtest_runs(suite, name) if suite and dot and name else None
        if kind == "node":
            path = rest.partition("#")[0]
            return self.node_runs(path) if path else None
        if kind == "py":
            path, sep, name = rest.partition("::")
            return self.py_runs(path) if path and sep and name else None
        if kind == "cargo":
            m = CARGO_RE.match(ref)
            return self.cargo_runs(m.group(1)) if m else None
        return None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- the debt (rules T1, T2, T3)
def missing_test_violations(root: Path) -> list:
    """T1 for every hook whose entry has no `test`, T2 for every anchor entry without `anchor` or `why`, T3 for every
    hook whose `test` no round runs, from the series merged with the committed hand fields (so a missing file makes
    every hook a T1). A declared exception (G35) has none of them."""
    found = []
    rounds = _Rounds(root)
    for folder in folders(root):
        for e in expected(root, folder):
            if is_declared_exception(e):
                continue
            text = e["line"] or ("<%s> %s" % (e["kind"], e.get("removed", "")))
            if not e.get("test") and not is_untestable(e):
                found.append(hc.Violation("T1", folder, e["file"], e["function"], text,
                                          hc._key("T1", folder, e["file"], e["function"], text)))
            elif isinstance(e["test"], str) and e["test"] and rounds.runs(e["test"]) is False:
                found.append(hc.Violation("T3", folder, e["file"], e["function"], text,
                                          hc._key("T3", folder, e["file"], e["function"], text), e["test"]))
            if e["form"] == "anchor" and not (e.get("anchor") and e.get("why")):
                found.append(hc.Violation("T2", folder, e["file"], e["function"], text,
                                          hc._key("T2", folder, e["file"], e["function"], text)))
    return hc._unique(found)


def summary_line(counts: dict) -> str:
    return "%d HOOKS.json files, %d entries" % (len(counts), sum(counts.values()))

